<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jakob Erhardt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1823">1823</a>–<a href="/years/1901">1901</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Johann Jakob Erhardt</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jakob Erhardt was a German missionary of the Church Missionary Society who worked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-ludwig-krapf">Johann Krapf</a> on the East African coast from 1849. He visited the Shambaa kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimweri-ye-nyumbai">Kimweri ye Nyumbai</a> and studied local languages. In 1856 he drew a map of a great inland sea based on caravan reports, which helped inspire Burton and Speke's expedition to the lakes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jakob-erhardt.jpg" alt="Jakob Erhardt" width="320" height="484" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: n.n. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johann-jakob-ehrhardt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Erhardt was born on 17 April 1823 in Bönnigheim, then in the Kingdom of Württemberg, the son of a master tailor, and was apprenticed to a cooper. He worked with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietism">Pietists</a> of Bönnigheim, then trained as a missionary with the Basel Mission until 1846. From 1846 to 1848 he studied with the Church Missionary Society in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, where he was ordained in 1848.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 10 June 1849 Erhardt and John Wagner reached the Rabbai Mpia mission station near Mombasa, joining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-ludwig-krapf">Johann Ludwig Krapf</a> and Johannes Rebmann; Wagner died on 1 August. In the spring of 1850 Erhardt and Krapf sailed by dhow down the coast via Tanga, Kilwa, Lindi and Mikindani to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-delgado">Cape Delgado</a>, unarmed and with few escorts, and mapped the mouth of the Pangani River and the deltas of the Rufiji and Ruvuma.</p><p>In September 1853 he reached Vugha in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usambara-mountains">Usambara Mountains</a>, capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimweri-ye-nyumbai">Kimweri ye Nyumbai</a>, and spent three months with the king. He saw two witches brought in and executed, and recorded the repulse of a Maasai raid at Mazinde by the Shambaa under Semboja, Kimweri's son, and their allies. In 1854 he spent six months at Tanga, where caravans gathered for the interior, studying the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shambaa-people">Shambala</a> language and hearing of Ukerewe (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-victoria">Lake Victoria</a>), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tanganyika">Lake Tanganyika</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-malawi">Lake Nyasa</a> and the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujiji">Ujiji</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Struck that travellers setting out from different points of the coast had all come to an inland sea, Erhardt worked out with Rebmann that an enormous valley and inland sea lay where geographers had supposed mountains. Their map showed the three lakes as one great S-shaped lake. It appeared in the Calwer Missionsblatt and the Church Missionary Intelligencer, and its reports of snow on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-kilimanjaro">Mount Kilimanjaro</a> and Mount Kenya, so near the equator, caused much controversy. Known as the slug map, which Burton called the Mombas Mission Map, it led the British government and the Royal Geographical Society to send <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-francis-burton">Richard Francis Burton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hanning-speke">John Hanning Speke</a> to find whether the lakes were the source of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>.</p><p>Poor health ended his work in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>, and he served in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from 1856 to 1891. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a> on 14 August 1901. A street in Bönnigheim, the Jakob-Erhardt-Straße, bears his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jakob Erhardt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Missionaries Johann Krapf and <b>Erhardt</b> visited his court.</blockquote><a href="/state/shambaa-kingdom">Shambaa Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bönnigheim, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.04,9.1&amp;year=1873" aria-label="Bönnigheim on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Jakob Erhardt"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5508 5897h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5508 5897h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.04,9.1&amp;year=1873">What was here in 1873? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
